WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.078
Writing Docs and Proposals.

2
00:00:02.358 --> 00:00:06.454
Day 66 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Workplace phase.

3
00:00:08.204 --> 00:00:09.228
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.508 --> 00:00:19.556
Today's goal: write design docs, proposals and READMEs that busy people actually read, so you can get decisions made and influence without being in every meeting.

5
00:00:20.906 --> 00:00:21.759
Part 1.

6
00:00:22.039 --> 00:00:23.981
Why writing matters for engineers.

7
00:00:25.231 --> 00:00:27.641
Senior engineers influence through writing.

8
00:00:27.921 --> 00:00:31.633
A clear one-page proposal reaches more people than any meeting.

9
00:00:31.913 --> 00:00:35.284
It's also how remote and global teams make decisions.

10
00:00:36.634 --> 00:00:37.509
Part 2.

11
00:00:37.789 --> 00:00:39.239
The one-page proposal.

12
00:00:40.489 --> 00:00:41.407
Step 1:

13
00:00:41.687 --> 00:00:46.785
Summary (TL;DR): the problem, your recommendation and the ask, in 3 lines.

14
00:00:47.065 --> 00:00:47.983
Step 2:

15
00:00:48.263 --> 00:00:52.572
Problem: what's wrong today, with evidence (numbers, incidents, user pain).

16
00:00:52.852 --> 00:00:53.769
Step 3:

17
00:00:54.049 --> 00:00:54.924
Options:

18
00:00:55.204 --> 00:00:58.617
2 to 3 options with pros, cons, cost and risk.

19
00:00:58.897 --> 00:00:59.857
Step 4:

20
00:01:00.137 --> 00:01:02.569
Recommendation: which option, and why.

21
00:01:02.849 --> 00:01:03.809
Step 5:

22
00:01:04.089 --> 00:01:08.015
Plan and ask: timeline, people, the decision you need, and by when.

23
00:01:09.365 --> 00:01:10.261
Part 3.

24
00:01:10.541 --> 00:01:11.970
Design doc template.

25
00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:14.799
Here is the MARKDOWN code.

26
00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:18.748
It runs to 32 lines; the full listing is in the chapter.

27
00:01:19.028 --> 00:01:19.903
Design:

28
00:01:20.183 --> 00:01:21.889
Delivery slot reservation.

29
00:01:22.169 --> 00:01:22.895
Author:

30
00:01:23.175 --> 00:01:24.497
Ashish, Reviewers:

31
00:01:24.777 --> 00:01:26.249
Meena, Arun, Status:

32
00:01:26.529 --> 00:01:27.873
Draft, Decision by:

33
00:01:28.153 --> 00:01:29.113
20 Nov.

34
00:01:29.393 --> 00:01:30.801
Context and problem.

35
00:01:33.651 --> 00:01:34.569
Part 4.

36
00:01:34.849 --> 00:01:35.915
Writing tips.

37
00:01:37.165 --> 00:01:38.531
Here is a quick table:

38
00:01:38.811 --> 00:01:39.835
Do, and Avoid.

39
00:01:40.115 --> 00:01:41.480
Put the summary first:

40
00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:43.744
Building up to the point on page 3.

41
00:01:44.024 --> 00:01:45.901
Use headings, bullets, tables:

42
00:01:46.181 --> 00:01:47.312
Long paragraphs.

43
00:01:47.592 --> 00:01:50.067
Show evidence: numbers, links, quotes:

44
00:01:50.347 --> 00:01:51.904
Everyone knows this is slow.

45
00:01:52.184 --> 00:01:53.848
Be explicit about trade-offs:

46
00:01:54.128 --> 00:01:56.240
Presenting only your favourite option.

47
00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.229
End with open questions and a decision date:

48
00:01:59.509 --> 00:02:01.301
Docs that never get decided.

49
00:02:01.581 --> 00:02:03.501
Write for someone new to the topic:

50
00:02:03.781 --> 00:02:06.491
Unexplained acronyms and internal jargon.

51
00:02:07.841 --> 00:02:08.801
Part 5.

52
00:02:09.081 --> 00:02:11.491
Asking for and handling review comments.

53
00:02:12.741 --> 00:02:15.707
Could you review the slot-reservation doc by Thursday?

54
00:02:15.987 --> 00:02:19.912
I'm mainly looking for feedback on the Redis option and the rollout plan.

55
00:02:20.192 --> 00:02:21.429
Reply to comments:

56
00:02:21.709 --> 00:02:26.317
Good point, I've added it to Risks. / I considered that, but chose B because...

57
00:02:26.597 --> 00:02:30.203
What do you think? / Let's discuss this one in the review meeting.

58
00:02:31.553 --> 00:02:32.491
Part 6.

59
00:02:32.771 --> 00:02:35.673
Conversation: getting a decision from a proposal.

60
00:02:36.923 --> 00:02:38.949
Here is how the conversation goes.

61
00:02:39.229 --> 00:02:40.616
Thanks for joining.

62
00:02:40.896 --> 00:02:43.541
Did everyone get a chance to read the proposal?

63
00:02:43.821 --> 00:02:44.675
Yes.

64
00:02:44.955 --> 00:02:48.261
I like option B, but I'm worried about Redis going down.

65
00:02:48.541 --> 00:02:49.480
Good point.

66
00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:55.029
If Redis is down, we fall back to no reservation, which is how it works today.

67
00:02:55.309 --> 00:02:57.443
I've added that to the Risks section.

68
00:02:57.723 --> 00:02:58.853
What's the cost?

69
00:03:00.203 --> 00:03:01.803
The conversation continues.

70
00:03:02.083 --> 00:03:07.694
Two engineers for two sprints, and about 6,000 rupeesa month for the Redis cluster.

71
00:03:07.974 --> 00:03:09.659
And the expected impact?

72
00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:19.113
We expect slot failures to drop from 8% to under 1%, which is roughly 1,500 more successful orders a week.

73
00:03:19.393 --> 00:03:20.481
Sounds good.

74
00:03:20.761 --> 00:03:22.062
Any objections?...

75
00:03:22.342 --> 00:03:23.195
No?

76
00:03:23.475 --> 00:03:25.246
Then let's go with option B.

77
00:03:26.596 --> 00:03:28.196
The conversation continues.

78
00:03:28.476 --> 00:03:29.223
Great.

79
00:03:29.503 --> 00:03:33.535
I'll update the doc status to Approved and create the tickets today.

80
00:03:34.885 --> 00:03:35.823
Part 7.

81
00:03:36.103 --> 00:03:37.021
Practice.

82
00:03:38.271 --> 00:03:45.033
Write the 3-line TL;DR for a change you'd like to make in your team: problem, recommendation, ask.

83
00:03:46.383 --> 00:03:47.258
Part 8.

84
00:03:47.538 --> 00:03:48.583
Speak today.

85
00:03:49.833 --> 00:03:53.460
Add a TL;DR to the top of one document you've written.

86
00:03:53.740 --> 00:03:56.812
Ask one colleague for review with a specific question.

87
00:03:58.162 --> 00:03:59.698
That's the end of this lesson.

88
00:03:59.978 --> 00:04:04.650
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

89
00:04:04.930 --> 00:04:11.031
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

